  'title'=>'How to Use Freshdesk: Setup, Tickets and Automation','seo_title'=>'How to Use Freshdesk: Setup, Tickets and Automation',
  'description'=>'Set up Freshdesk support email, ticket workflows, automations, knowledge content and reporting with a practical checklist based on official documentation.',
  'url'=>'/freshdesk/how-to-use/','date'=>'2026-10-28','status'=>'published','primary_keyword'=>'how to use Freshdesk',
  'content'=><<<'HTML'
<h2>Before you start</h2>
<p>Decide whether you need standalone Freshdesk or Freshdesk Omni. The standalone product is positioned around email-first support, while Omni is a separate product for broader channels. The plans and feature availability differ, so start with the <a href="https://www.freshworks.com/freshdesk/pricing/">official Freshdesk pricing page</a> and confirm the product name before you invite agents.</p>
<p>Have an account administrator available, access to the mailbox you want to use for support, and permission to change its forwarding or mail settings if required. Prepare a short list of ticket types, priority rules, escalation owners and reports before configuring automation.</p>
<h2>1. Choose the plan and set up the team</h2>
<p>Review Growth, Pro and Enterprise against the features you need. Growth lists ticketing, shared inbox, customer portal, knowledge base and reporting. Pro adds multilingual help desk, custom dashboards, intelligent routing and multiple SLA policies. Enterprise adds skill-based routing, sandbox, audit logs and allowed-domain or IP controls. These details were checked October 28, 2026 on the <a href="https://www.freshworks.com/freshdesk/pricing/">official pricing page</a>.</p>
<p>Invite only the people who need to work tickets at the start. Define who can assign tickets, change rules, manage email and update the knowledge base. Keep a note of which admin owns billing and data export, because these tasks may need account-level permissions.</p>
<h2>2. Configure the support email</h2>
<p>Freshdesk creates a default support email when you open an account. The official guide says emails sent to a configured support address become tickets. To set up a business address, follow the <a href="https://support.freshdesk.com/support/solutions/articles/37541-setting-up-your-support-email">Freshdesk support email guide</a>, updated July 3, 2026.</p>
<ol><li>Sign in with an administrator account.</li><li>Open Admin and go to Channels, then Email. Use the current guide if your account shows a different label.</li><li>Add the support address and follow the verification steps for your mail server.</li><li>If you use an external mailbox, configure the forwarding or mail settings required by the guide.</li><li>Send a test message from an outside address and confirm that it creates a ticket with the right requester and reply address.</li></ol>
<p>Do not switch all customer mail at once. Test replies, forwarding, threading and sender details with a small group first. The <a href="https://support.freshdesk.com/support/solutions/articles/50000009268-overview-of-email-channel">email channel overview</a>, updated January 21, 2026, explains default and additional support addresses.</p>
<h2>3. Define ticket categories and ownership</h2>
<p>Use categories that match the work the team actually handles. A small set such as billing, account access, product issue and feature request is easier to maintain than a long list that overlaps. Decide who owns each category, how urgent work is marked and when a ticket moves to another team.</p>
<p>Write down the expected next step for a ticket that has no clear owner. For example, assign it to a triage queue and review unassigned work at set times. A routing rule is useful only when the team knows what should happen when the rule does not match.</p>
<h2>4. Add automation carefully</h2>
<p>Freshdesk's ticket-update rules listen for events, check conditions and take actions. The official guide gives examples such as reopening a resolved ticket after a customer replies, assigning an agent when a note is added by a third party, or sending a survey when a ticket is resolved. See <a href="https://support.freshdesk.com/support/solutions/articles/99047-setting-up-automation-rules-to-run-on-ticket-updates">Creating a new automation rule to run on ticket updates</a>, updated May 11, 2026.</p>
<ol><li>Open Admin, then Workflows, then Automation Rules.</li><li>Select the Ticket Updates tab and create one rule for a clear event.</li><li>Choose the conditions that must match and the action to run.</li><li>Keep the rule name specific so another admin can tell what it does.</li><li>Test the rule with a sample ticket and inspect the result before adding more rules.</li><li>Review rule order because Freshdesk says rules execute from top to bottom.</li></ol>
<p>Avoid several rules that update the same field in different ways. Keep an exception path for tickets that do not match your conditions, and review the rule statistics after the team starts using the workflow.</p>
<h2>5. Prepare the knowledge base and customer portal</h2>
<p>Use the knowledge base for answers that do not need a person each time. Write one article for one common question, use the same names as the interface, and put the steps in the order a customer should follow. Include a clear point where the customer should stop and contact support if the steps do not work.</p>
<p>Before publishing a large set of articles, assign an owner and a review date. Remove duplicate instructions and update pages when a product change makes the steps wrong. The <a href="https://support.freshdesk.com/support/solutions">Freshdesk Help Center</a> includes sections for knowledge base management, portal setup, workflows, reporting and integrations.</p>
<h2>6. Set routing, SLAs and reporting</h2>
<p>Choose response and resolution targets that the team can meet. If the process needs multilingual help desk, custom dashboards, intelligent routing or multiple SLA policies, confirm the selected plan includes those controls. Enterprise's published list includes skill-based routing and additional administration features. Do not design a process around a feature until the plan is confirmed.</p>
<p>Review volume, unassigned tickets, response time and resolution patterns together. A quick first reply does not prove a ticket was solved quickly. Check the queue that grows, the category with repeat contacts and the knowledge articles that could prevent avoidable requests.</p>
<h2>7. Check integrations and permissions</h2>
<p>Start with the apps the team needs to see customer context or complete a support task. Check the official integration listing and the current plan requirements. Do not assume every app supports every field or action. Give each user the permissions needed for the job, and restrict access to billing, account configuration and automation changes.</p>
<h2>8. Plan exports and migration</h2>
<p>Before you move from Freshdesk or change plans, decide what data must be preserved. The <a href="https://support.freshdesk.com/support/solutions/articles/225158">ticket export guide</a> describes filtered ticket-list exports to CSV or Excel. The <a href="https://support.freshdesk.com/support/solutions/articles/225487-how-do-i-export-my-helpdesk-data-">account data export guide</a> covers the separate account-level path. Do not assume a filtered list contains every conversation, attachment, archived record, report or custom field you need.</p>
<ol><li>List tickets, replies, attachments, users, companies, custom fields, knowledge articles, macros and automation rules that must be kept.</li><li>Check the destination system's import docs and record fields that cannot be mapped.</li><li>Export a small sample and inspect timestamps, requester details, attachments and character encoding.</li><li>Test the import in a non-production workspace if the destination supports it.</li><li>Compare record counts and assign an owner to any data that cannot be imported directly.</li><li>Agree on a cutover time and how the team will handle requests that arrive during the switch.</li></ol>
<p>Keep the source account until the destination owner confirms that the required records are present and accessible. A new ticket queue is not a full migration if important history is missing.</p>
<h2>Common mistakes and fixes</h2>
<ul><li><strong>Email does not create a ticket:</strong> confirm the support address, verification status, forwarding rules and mailbox permissions. Follow the official <a href="https://support.freshdesk.com/support/solutions/articles/37541-setting-up-your-support-email">email setup troubleshooting steps</a>.</li><li><strong>Replies create duplicate tickets:</strong> review email threading settings and the way the sender or ticket identifier is handled. Use the <a href="https://support.freshdesk.com/support/solutions/articles/50000009268-overview-of-email-channel">email channel documentation</a>.</li><li><strong>An automation changes the wrong ticket:</strong> check rule order, conditions and overlapping actions. Test one rule at a time.</li><li><strong>A report does not show a needed field:</strong> confirm plan limits and dashboard options before redesigning the workflow.</li><li><strong>The bill is higher than expected:</strong> review agent count, billing cycle, AI sessions, Copilot licenses and connector usage.</li></ul>
<h2>Related Freshdesk guides</h2>
<p>Use the <a href="/freshdesk/">Freshdesk hub</a> for the product overview, <a href="/freshdesk/pricing/">pricing guide</a> for plan costs, <a href="/freshdesk/review/">review</a> for fit and trade-offs, and <a href="/freshdesk/alternatives/">alternatives guide</a> if the workflow no longer matches the product. The <a href="/compare/zendesk-vs-freshdesk/">Zendesk vs Freshdesk comparison</a> covers the closest direct alternative.</p>
